A||||15||jeffndebrus||Thermostat||||1||
Z||000000||jeffndebrus||11-08-2001||11:49 AM||||During some routine maintenance on my 2.8, I found that there is no thermostat in my car.<BR>Should I continue to run it without ???<BR>Or put one in-- what temp? and what about the drill holes I have read about people putting in them??||||reg||1
Z||000001||jeffndebrus||11-08-2001||12:04 PM||||And does anyone know why the previous owner may have done this??<P>Also he sealed the cap with silicone??<BR>Possibly because the surface is a little rough with corrosion.||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnt671||11-08-2001||12:15 PM||||Put one in.  The car will run better.  Surprised you aren't having drivability problems and poor gas mileage.  <p>[This message has been edited by johnt671 (edited 11-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||camon||11-08-2001||12:24 PM||||I agree use one.<P>I rescently replaced mine and ran it like 1 hour without any and it ran poorly. So I ran right out and got a new one.<P>The stock thermostat temp is a 195! Atleast that is the correct one for the 87 GT. If unsure goto auto part store (autozone, Advanced Auto ect...) and ask them...they can tell you!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000004||Voytek||11-08-2001||01:53 PM||||Yeah, stock is 195 but I feel the 180 runs best.  I've had the 165 - way too cold, the 195 - temp goes up faster in slow traffic, and now the 180.  The 180 keeps the temperature just right.<P>W/o a thermostat, your cooling fluid circulates fast.  What this means is that your car takes a long time to warm up (all this time giving you bad gas mileage).  Then, when the engine does warm up, the antifreeze doesn't have enough time to cool in the radiator.  It circulates faster than it should and your car may run hot.<P>Get one.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jefrysuko||11-08-2001||03:11 PM||||You might want to replace that cap at the same time.  They only cost a couple bucks and you can get them at just about any autoparts store.<P>I have seen several occasions where someone appeared to leave the thermostat out due to having interference problems with the cap.  One case someone had a hard time opening up the thermostat housing so they beat on the cap with something and caved it in.  obvioustly the thermostat then would not fit so they left it out.  Also rust collects in the thermostat housing which will not allow the thermostat to go all the way down in it's seat properly.  In this case you need to remove that rust so that the thermostat is completely seated and then everything will fit.||||reg||1
Z||000006||pherder||11-08-2001||04:11 PM||||On another thermostat problem ... has anyone else experience having the thermostat "handle" collapsing after high engine rpms?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>I just had my second experience.  It shows up as the car never warming up since the thermostat isn't sealing anymore.<P>Paul||||reg||5
Z||000007||johnt671||11-08-2001||06:29 PM||||I second the 180 degree thermostat.  I had one in mine for 4 years now.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||11-08-2001||08:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>Then, when the engine does warm up, the antifreeze doesn't have enough time to cool in the radiator.  It circulates faster than it should and your car may run hot.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><BR>Uh???<BR>By speeding up the flow, you are actually improving the overall cooling... More water gets exposed the larger surface area of the rad... Better cooling<P>That aside, replace the thermo... It'll run weird/inefficiently, otherwise||||reg||11
Z||000009||Kasdan||11-08-2001||08:13 PM||||Mine runs hot with a 180...so i took it out alltogether, runs at like 140-160 most of the time, and still has lots of power||||reg||1
Z||000010||camon||11-09-2001||01:28 AM||||Kasdan:<P>Amazing......<BR>When I ran mine without a thermostat (for like an hour) it ran aweful. Started the car left it warm for about 3 minutes went to pull off and it would die. The only way I got it to move was to keep the r's up. then it was even hesitant. Heck mine never even warmed-up during that hour (got to the first line and that was it).<P>Anyway....<BR>I'm still using the stock 195 but hearing the positive feed back from most I am gonna try the 180. Is the 180 available at most auto part stores (reason I ask is because at autozone I asked for a thermostat and they gave me the 195 and didn't even ask if I wanted anything other then the 195)???<P>I also had a therostat calapse (the thin metal grab thingy...got all bent up). I bought another new theromstat and cap and it is fine now!  <P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000011||Kasdan||11-09-2001||06:52 AM||||Yeah....did i mention im running a brand new long block?  crazy huh?||||reg||1
Z||000012||GodSend||11-09-2001||11:32 AM||||Just a quick question guys.  I am running a 180 term in my car, but I have an emissions test coming up next month.  I have an extra 195 laying around..  Will the 195 (hotter engine) help reduce my emissions?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Voytek||11-09-2001||05:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> <BR>Uh???<BR>By speeding up the flow, you are actually improving the overall cooling... More water gets exposed the larger surface area of the rad... Better cooling<P>That aside, replace the thermo... It'll run weird/inefficiently, otherwise[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Umm, larger surface area???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> The area within the radiator doesn't change, whether your fluid is circulating fast or slow, it is still the same exposure.<P>What happens is this: the hot fluid is transported in the pipes to the radiator where it has a chance to cool off.  The thermostat regulates how fast this will happen.  At normal operating conditions, the thermostat opens and closes to maintain a steady temperature.  Because of the restricted flow, the coolant in the radiator has more time to cool off prior to re-entering the engine.  Fast flow = recirculation of hot antifreeze and not enough time to cool.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Scott||11-09-2001||07:13 PM||||camon- I work at AutoZone, I'm sure I could find you 180* thermostat in a store.  Tell them to look harder, someone there should be able to find you one. Look for the Stant or lifetime guaranteed part, it'll be the best for the money; they'll also have a Hypertech unit, but it'll cost ~$12 and they might have to order it.  I've used the 195, 180, & 160 themrostats in my car, and I like the 180 best for everyday driving.<BR>Good luck,<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000015||Two88GTs||11-09-2001||09:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B>On another thermostat problem ... has anyone else experience having the thermostat "handle" collapsing after high engine rpms?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>I just had my second experience.  It shows up as the car never warming up since the thermostat isn't sealing anymore.<P>Paul</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I just went throught the same thing myself about a month ago.  The car would never warm up to what I consider a normal temperature unless the car sat in traffic.  Finally opened up the thermostat cap to find a thermostat that looked as if Godzilla had stomped on it.<BR>||||reg||1
